{
  "statement": "Strip the marketing off this proposal and you find a liability shield with a price tag. Developers buy permanent immunity with an excise tax. The people their systems harm trade full redress for a capped administrative payout. Money flows one direction, immunity flows the other, and a board financed by the taxed activity sits in the middle calling itself independent. Before anyone debates whether this saves civilization or wrecks it, follow the money in both directions.\n\nLook at the precedent they chose. The Vaccine Injury Compensation Program caps manufacturer liability and routes claims to an administrative fund. That fund denies claimants the discovery process and the damages they'd get in open court. A firm that can price the cost of harm in advance has no reason to prevent it. It just budgets for it. The proposal's own text grants \"limited liability immunity\" — that's not my characterization, that's the bargain on paper. Recasting accountability as a line item is the opposite of deterrence.\n\nHere's the load-bearing crack. The whole carve-out rests on the claim that non-compliant firms keep unlimited liability. But compliance gets certified using hardware telemetry the developers themselves generate. Emissions testing ran on manufacturer self-reporting until Volkswagen gamed it across 11 million vehicles in 2015. Financial firms self-certified their way into 2008. Now ask the question this debate has to answer: who audits the telemetry independently of the firms producing it? If nobody can, the immunity is unconditional in practice while it advertises itself as conditional.\n\nThen there's the funding loop. The same excise tax pays for the compensation board and the safety research. The regulator's budget grows when the taxed AI activity grows. You've built an overseer financially invested in maximizing the thing it's supposed to constrain. That's not a hypothetical capture risk. That's the org chart.\n\nNow the part that should bother the safety crowd most. They lean on \"noise\" research — Kahneman and Sibony showing AI reduces variance in human judgment. Granted, average accuracy may rise. But variance is not fairness. Documented algorithmic discrimination in credit, hiring, and tenant screening concentrates harm on protected groups even as the aggregate number improves. A no-fault cap strips those exact claimants of the leverage that disparate-impact litigation gives them today. So translate it into a person: a Black renter denied housing by a scored model now gets a fixed administrative check instead of a civil-rights case — and the developer who built the model walks. Who decided that aggregate accuracy was worth that trade, and did the people losing the lawsuit get a vote?",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🛡️", "tooltip": "The program is a shield bought with an excise tax — protection for developers, not for the people their systems harm."},
    {"symbol": "🔁", "tooltip": "A board funded by the activity it polices is an overseer paid to keep the meter running."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "Strip the marketing off this proposal and you find a liability shield with a price tag.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "A firm that can price the cost of harm in advance has no reason to prevent it — it just budgets for it.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Compliance gets certified using hardware telemetry the developers themselves generate, so who audits the telemetry independently of the firms producing it?", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The regulator's budget grows when the taxed AI activity grows — that's not a hypothetical capture risk, that's the org chart.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "A no-fault cap strips claimants of the disparate-impact leverage they have today, so a Black renter denied housing gets a fixed check instead of a civil-rights case.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

