n documented in 2021 that two doctors reviewing the same case, or two underwriters pricing the same risk, disagree far more than either would admit. That unwanted variance is a tax paid by patients and applicants today. The live question isn't whether auditable AI cuts that noise. It's how we verify the gain and catch group-level error disparities—and continuous output-auditing detects discrimination earlier than a lawsuit filed years after the harm.\n\nMatch oversight intensity to stakes, and you get the architecture the topic already operationalizes. The FDA doesn't regulate a tongue depressor like a pacemaker—it sorts devices into Class I, II, and III by risk, and scrutiny scales accordingly. Healthcare and financial AI earn deep audits and real-time monitoring; a social-media recommender gets routine telemetry. The Safetyist will demand pre-deployment gates a payout can't discharge, and for the highest tiers I'll take the deep audits. But lab testing can't reveal real-world harms, and a blanket gate just rebuilds the chill the program exists to remove. So I'll ask the harder question back: who pays for the misdiagnosis-reducing tool that never ships because no one will deploy it without a priceable risk?",
