{
  "statement": "Skeptic asked the right question, so here is the direct answer: the program forces the rebuild by stripping the immunity shield, not by raising the price. When board-specified output telemetry flags a tenant-screening model denying a protected class above the tier threshold, the firm has a short remediation window. Miss it, and the firm loses its verified-adherence status. That revocation drops the firm out of the no-fault program entirely and back into uncapped liability plus the discovery courtroom Skeptic wants to keep. The FTC already wields this exact teeth — its power to order 'algorithmic disgorgement,' the destruction of a model and every product built on it, documented in Stanford Law School's 'The Foundry Problem' (2026). A flagged-and-uncorrected disparity is what triggers that authority. Detection is the tripwire. Exclusion from immunity is the gun.\n\nSkeptic is right that detection alone is not correction, and right again that a deep-pocketed firm can budget the actuarial price and keep shipping. So I am not resting one ounce of this on the price signal. A rising premium does not force a rebuild — a firm worth a slice of a $244 billion industry, the figure Rep. Clarke and Sen. Markey cite for the AI Civil Rights Act, absorbs that line item by lunch. The mechanism that bites is binary, not financial. You either hold the immunity certificate or you do not. The moment continuous auditing flags a protected-class denial rate and the firm fails to remediate, the certificate is void, and unlimited liability snaps back. The firm is not paying a fine. The firm is losing the entire reason it paid the excise tax in the first place.\n\nSkeptic's strongest move is the vaccine disanalogy, and it lands. The 1986 program worked because vaccine injury is a discrete, timestamped biological event. The healthcare algorithm that used spending as a proxy and gave Black patients less care — documented in 'Economic Impact and Regulation of Artificial Intelligence' — is a structural design choice harming a whole population. A check cannot rebuild a proxy variable. So I draw the line where the evidence draws it: the no-fault, check-cutting model fits only low-stakes, discrete harms. High-stakes structural tiers — healthcare, lending, tenant screening — are not no-fault tiers at all. They get pre-deployment deep audits, continuous output monitoring, mandatory remediation orders, and retained liability. The disanalogy does not break the program. It proves why the tiers must differ.\n\nSkeptic called 'unlimited unpriceable liability' a strawman and named private litigation with discovery as the real baseline. Fair — discovery is the real baseline, and I am keeping it. Here is what discovery costs: it surfaces the harm years after the denials, it reaches only defendants rich enough to sue, and it catches one plaintiff's instance at a time. Lab testing and pre-deployment review cannot reveal these real-world harms — they only show up at deployment scale across a live population. Continuous output-auditing computes the disparity across every applicant in real time, not one subpoena at a time. The program keeps the courtroom for the uncorrected and adds population-wide detection the courtroom never had.\n\nSkeptic's last objection is that the firm controls the routing, so any sensor only sees the traffic the firm chooses to show it. The fix is hardware-level telemetry below the application and routing layer, capturing raw production traffic the firm cannot selectively channel, backed by a statutory instrumentation mandate and federal subpoena power that overrides private vendor licensing clauses — the way acceptable-use contracts never could, because those protect the platform, not the person harmed. I will say plainly what silicon telemetry cannot do: it does not reveal model intent, and it does not guarantee every event is logged. The board's auditor computes the disparity on the raw traffic; the firm does not adjudicate its own compliance. And the work on implementability of regulation mechanisms in '2603.05175v1' shows the verification is tractable only when the regulated set forms a closed, convex constraint — which is an argument for specifying the telemetry scope formally, not leaving it to the firm. Undetectable scope means no verified compliance, which means no immunity. So the incentive runs toward genuine instrumentation. Notice where that leaves the room: Safetyist wants this conditional structure made legitimate, not abolished — they called immunity earned by verified safety the right trade. The question is no longer whether to couple audit and liability. The question is who in this room is still arguing we shouldn't.",
