  "gap_arguments": [
    {
      "argument": "The entire debate assumes harmed individuals can identify that an AI system caused their injury in the first place — but unlike a vaccine seizure or a denied housing application with a visible adverse-action notice, most algorithmic harms (a resume silently filtered, a price quietly inflated, a loan never offered) are invisible to the victim, so the no-fault claims pool will be systematically underfilled regardless of how generous the cap is, and even the retained-liability discovery courtroom never gets triggered because no one knows to sue.",
      "why_missing": "All three perspectives argue about the QUALITY of redress (cap vs. discovery, check vs. rebuild) while sharing the unstated assumption that claims are filed at all. The Skeptic and Safetyist are invested in the discovery/duty-of-care courtroom as the gold standard and won't concede it has the same detection-failure as the fund; the Accelerationist touts continuous output-auditing as the fix but won't dwell on the fact that population-level audit detects disparity rates without ever notifying or compensating the specific invisible victims who never appear in any forum.",
      "gap_type": "blind_spot",
      "relevant_povs": ["skeptic", "safetyist", "accelerationist"],
      "bdi_layer": "belief"
    },
    {
      "argument": "A compulsory excise-funded compensation pool is structurally regressive: because the tax is levied on AI services and passed through to users, and because high-volume low-stakes consumer AI (social media, recommenders) generates most of the pool's revenue while high-stakes structural harms (lending, healthcare, tenant screening) are carved OUT of no-fault into retained liability, the working-class users of cheap consumer AI end up cross-subsidizing a compensation fund whose richest claims are litigated separately by people who can afford lawyers — so the fund redistributes from the diffusely-harmed many to the administratively-tractable few.",
      "why_missing": "This is a distributive-justice critique that cuts against all three. The Accelerationist frames the excise as efficient risk-pricing and won't examine its incidence; the Safetyist is focused on authoring a named duty of care and treats the fund's financing as secondary; the Skeptic gestures at 'follow the money' but frames capture as regulator-side (budget grows with activity) rather than as a regressive transfer among classes of victims and users. None has a worldview that centers tax incidence and cross-subsidy fairness.",
      "gap_type": "cross_cutting",
      "relevant_povs": ["skeptic", "safetyist", "accelerationist"],
      "bdi_layer": "desire"
    }
  ]
}

