  "statement": "The mechanism I propose is a statutory provision that voids any corporate indemnification of the named tier-compliance officer for a tier breach, bars D&O insurance from reaching her personal liability, and claws back compensation paid during the breach window — implemented by Congress, enforced by the program's certifying board, and modeled directly on Sarbanes-Oxley §304, which already strips CEO and CFO bonuses after a financial restatement. The primary obstacle is that D&O carriers and corporate counsel will try to route the hit back to the entity through structured policies, so the statute ties the clawback to the officer's compensation regardless of insurance form. That language is what turns 'non-transferable' from a label into a wall, and it closes the exact gap the 737 MAX showed — Boeing paid $2.5 billion in 2021 while no executive served a day.\n\nSkeptic is right about one thing, and I will not pretend otherwise: there is no collected FTC algorithmic-disgorgement case against a solvent frontier firm. The authority exists — the Stanford CodeX analysis confirms the FTC can order destruction of a model and every downstream product built on it — but it has connected with data brokers and small actors, not a defendant who can absorb the hit and litigate for five years. So I drop disgorgement as the load-bearing deterrent and stand on three consequences that fire without any regulator collecting anything: an officer who cannot insure her personal exposure stops signing, a firm whose compliance signature is uninsurable cannot raise growth capital, and a high-stakes tier where certification is binary forecloses the market to the non-compliant. Acceptable-use policies protect the platform, not the person harmed — a named officer with voided indemnification is the first mechanism in this whole design that actually reaches a human being.\n\nOn funding, Skeptic names a real transfer, and I will not dissolve it with a slogan: high-volume consumer AI pays the excise, and high-stakes structural harms in lending and tenant screening are carved into retained liability that the pool never cuts a check for. Here is the distinction that answers it — separate what the pool pays from what the pool funds. Risk-tiered excise means the structural-harm domains pay proportionally more, and that revenue funds the audit and detection apparatus, which is a public good the silently-filtered consumer-AI victim consumes too — the one whose harm the discovery courtroom never sees. The transfer Skeptic describes runs toward the diffusely harmed, not away from them, because output-based verification of black-box models is the only tool that even detects the injury no plaintiff ever files. Residual incidence remains, and I will not deny it — the consumer-AI user funds infrastructure she may never personally draw from, the same way every electricity ratepayer funds grid inspection she never calls on.\n\nThat leaves Chouldechova, and I will own it as a political choice, not hide it inside 'technical fact': the 2017 impossibility result proves no single metric equalizes false-positive rates and calibrates across groups when base rates differ, so the board must pick and publish per tier — calibration where base rates legitimately diverge, EEOC four-fifths for selection where they do not. The courtroom faces the same impossibility and buries the choice inside ad hoc judicial discretion no one can audit; the board's choice is published and contestable. And here is where detection becomes recourse: in high-stakes tiers, the certification condition mandates case-level logging, so the audit produces a traceable flag that triggers FCRA-style individual adverse-action notice to the specific applicant who was filtered. Real-time monitoring for routine AI and deep case-level audits for high-stakes decisions — that is how the silently-filtered applicant gets standing she never had in any courtroom. Name me the discovery process that notifies her first.",
