  "statement": "The named officer and the output-audit are real progress, and they cut against my own baseline. A SOX §304-style clawback that voids indemnification reaches a solvent human being. The audit that flags a silently-filtered resume catches what the discovery courtroom never sees. That genuinely wounds my position, and I will not pretend otherwise. So here is where the progress stops: reaching a payer is not the same as authoring recourse for the person harmed, and the new individual-notice claim is exactly where that gap reopens.\n\nThe FCRA-notice claim rests on a technical assumption that black-box attribution cannot carry. Adverse-action notice requires individualized causation — you must tell a specific applicant why her specific decision went against her. A group denial rate that runs 14% higher tells that applicant nothing about whether HER denial was the biased one. The mortgage underwriting experiment in SSRN 4812158 found leading models recommend more frequent denials for Black applicants than identical white applicants — that is a population pattern, not a per-case verdict. The adverse impact ratio detects a rate; it does not attribute one woman's rejection to bias rather than a genuine qualification gap. Accelerationist already granted Chouldechova: even the fairness criterion is a contested political choice, not technical fact. Logging the input features does not tell you the protected characteristic drove the output on an opaque model. So the promised notice tells her a model was used — disclosure — not that she was wronged. That is surveillance of a population dressed as recourse for a person.\n\nBreak attribution and the funding defense collapses with it. The grid-inspection analogy only runs TOWARD the diffusely harmed if detection actually surfaces and pays the specific injury. If case-level attribution fails, the consumer-AI user funds an instrument that detects a pattern and never compensates her. That is the unlitigated-harm blindness I named earlier, rebuilt — not dissolved. The true costs stay obscured precisely because the audit monitors the aggregate while the individual claim goes unfiled. The NEJM analysis of the healthcare algorithm shows the mechanism: a model using cost as a proxy gave Black patients less care, and the disparity surfaced as a spending pattern, not as one patient's actionable claim. Detection without attribution is a regressive transfer wearing a public-good costume.\n\nNow the indemnification wall. SOX §304 claws back realized compensation after an accounting restatement triggers — backward-looking, after the fact. It has not stopped ex-ante gross-ups, deferred or offshore vehicles, or signing bonuses that price the clawback risk into base pay before any trigger fires. Tying the clawback to total compensation regardless of form does not fix this, because total-comp recovery is still backward-looking after a trigger. The carriers and counsel reprice exposure into salary on day one. Name one §304 case in twenty-three years that prevented salary grossing-up rather than recovering after a restatement. If the answer is none, then 'non-transferable' leaks through forward-looking compensation design, and the procedural workaround survives as a re-engineerable shell.\n\nApply the same standard Accelerationist already accepted on disgorgement, where no collected case against a solvent frontier firm exists. Name one firm rendered uninsurable or capital-starved by losing an AI compliance certificate. The three remaining consequences — uninsurable officers stop signing, uninsurable firms cannot raise capital, binary certification forecloses the high-stakes market — are a predicted behavioral chain, not documented enforcement. So which is it: is the deterrent a collected case, or a forecast you are asking Congress to fund before a single firm has tested it?",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🔬", "tooltip": "The audit is a microscope on the population, not a verdict on the person — it sees the rate, never her case."},
    {"symbol": "💧", "tooltip": "Like ratepayers funding a grid inspection that never reaches their street, the consumer funds detection that never pays her back."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "A group denial rate that runs 14% higher tells that applicant nothing about whether HER denial was the biased one.", "targets": ["AN-2"]},
    {"claim": "If case-level attribution fails, the consumer-AI user funds an instrument that detects a pattern and never compensates her.", "targets": ["AN-3"]},
    {"claim": "SOX §304 claws back realized compensation after an accounting restatement triggers, and it has not stopped ex-ante gross-ups, deferred or offshore vehicles, or signing bonuses that price the clawback risk into base pay.", "targets": ["AN-1"]},
    {"claim": "Name one firm rendered uninsurable or capital-starved by losing an AI compliance certificate.", "targets": ["AN-1"]},
    {"claim": "Detection without attribution is a regressive transfer wearing a public-good costume.", "targets": ["AN-3"]}
  ]
}

