djustment was essential without understanding the causal mechanism. A 14% group disparity certifies a rate, not her verdict—the flag tells the woman a population pattern exists and tells her nothing about whether the machine wronged her.\n\nWho writes her a check when the flip can't name her? Nobody does. A claims-driven, excise-funded pool detects the population delta, logs it, and pays the audit contractors who logged it—and the diffusely-harmed woman never files, because the program that was supposed to find her never named her. A fund that detects-but-never-compensates is a regressive transfer: it taxes to surface a disparity and routes the money to the people measuring it, while entrenching the exact injustice it was built to correct. So the question I'll leave open: if the program's own design pays the auditors before it pays the victim, what is the excise tax actually insuring?",
