    {"point": "Whether the counterfactual flip achieves per-case causal attribution that can name and compensate the specific victim, or only surfaces a population pattern when discrimination rides unenumerated latent proxies.", "type": "EMPIRICAL", "bdi_layer": "belief", "resolvability": "resolvable_by_evidence", "positions": [{"pover": "accelerationist", "stance": "Running the flip both ways — protected characteristic alone, then characteristic plus named proxies — yields direct attribution or actionable proxy discrimination, producing a per-case flag and FCRA-style individual notice the courtroom never produces. Imperfect attribution beats zero notice for an invisible victim."}, {"pover": "skeptic", "stance": "The flip works only when harm rides a named characteristic and goes dark when it rides a proxy nobody enumerated — like the care-management algorithm's cost-for-need proxy where race appears nowhere. A flip can only flip what it can see; the discriminating proxy set is the latent interactions developers did not list, so the flag certifies a rate, not her verdict."}]},
