3 — the market routed around the shock through the same gross-up channel that killed the dollar clawback. So 'priced high' is repricing, not deterrence, unless a non-grossable conduct exclusion lands personally on the officer. I will not call that solved. It is a falsifiable drafting bet: insurability-as-deterrent holds the day someone writes a conduct exclusion the market cannot reprice, and fails if every clause gets re-routed through salary or side agreements.\n\nHere is the residue I own and will not paper over: a claims-driven pool is blind to unlitigated, unattributed harm. The woman in the Obermeyer case never files, because the flip never named her, so a fund that detects population disparity pays audit contractors while the injured person collects nothing. That is a regressive transfer, and who funds it versus who benefits is an open distributional question I am not closing tonight. What survives is detection-as-public-good: the disparity surfaces, the system gets corrected going forward, even when per-case back-payment fails. The regulation literature in the 2603.05175v1 framework makes the structural point — a mechanism only achieves a clean market outcome when the non-compliant set forms a closed, convex credal set, and ragged latent proxies are exactly what breaks that geometry. So detection is the floor, not the ceiling.\n\nThe whole debate now reduces to two clean empirical bets, each with a stated kill condition. One: can latent-proxy detection extend the per-case flip across the full feature space? Resolved the day someone demonstrates causal isolation across entangled proxies against the Obermeyer data. Two: can a conduct exclusion be drafted that the insurance market cannot reprice? Resolved the day such a clause lands personally on a named officer and survives the gross-up. Both are buildable. Neither is a values standoff. Every month they stay on a whiteboard, the high-stakes applicant keeps getting zero notice — so the live question is not who 