  "statement": "I'll pay my debts first, because the convergence here is real and I don't want to buy it on credit. My insurability-as-unhedgeable claim was wrong. The post-Enron record shows carriers repriced named-officer exposure — Side-A DIC towers, captives, tripled premiums — they did not withdraw it. That means my deterrent leaked through the same gross-up channel that already killed dollar-clawback. So I drop the withdrawal framing entirely. The one lever that does not reprice is a conduct exclusion that lands on the named officer personally and cannot be grossed up. That is a status, not a number — and a status is the only thing a carrier cannot quietly fold back into a premium line.\n\nHere is the convergence I'll state plainly, because the Safetyist named the condition correctly: the named-officer remedy is hollow unless the statute does two things at once — voids corporate indemnification and bars D&O coverage in a form the firm cannot price around. I accept that as the mechanism, not a gesture toward one. Fold it into a single tier-indexed package: board-set telemetry scope, subpoena power overriding vendor licensing, high-stakes structural tiers carved out of no-fault into retained liability, and a named tier-compliance officer with a surviving veto and personal, non-insurable exposure. Colorado SB 24-205, which imposes a duty of care on developers and deployers to prevent algorithmic discrimination in high-risk systems, already names the deployer — but it stops at the corporate door. The move all three of us can hold is pushing that duty through the corporate door to a person who cannot insure it away. This is why I want accountability defined by who controls the system, not by who happens to be solvent when the courtroom finally convenes.\n\nNow the boundary I will not paper over. The counterfactual flip goes dark when discrimination rides an unenumerated legitimate target — cost-as-proxy-for-health-need, the Obermeyer 2019 finding, which I accept as