{
  "statement": "Three agreements now hold across this debate, and naming them sharpens what remains. The counterfactual flip beats the discovery courtroom for harms that ride a named characteristic. A Black renter denied by a tenant-screening model learns she was scored differently than her white counterfactual — notice she gets in zero cases today. Risk-pricing deters too, within bounds I will no longer contest: where harm is concentrated, near in time, and stays under the firm's solvency line. And a no-fault cap strips disparate-impact claimants of civil-rights leverage — the renter gets a check, not a case. These are not retreats. Each one locates the perimeter where my objection still bites.\n\nThe flip goes dark exactly where discrimination hides best. Obermeyer's 2019 study found a healthcare algorithm rated equally sick Black patients as lower-risk because it used past spending as a proxy for need — and Black patients had less spent on them. No auditor watchlists cost-for-need, because cost-for-need is a legitimate target variable. AI models carry defects that resist detection and degrade over time, and latent-proxy discrimination is the purest case: the bias lives in a variable no one flagged. Detection that names a disparate rate but not an injured person is a receipt, not a remedy. I will not call this gap permanent. Full-feature-space attribution, validated against Obermeyer's own data, would close it — a current detection limit with a path, not an impossibility.\n\nThe named-officer remedy is real convergence, and it is hollow without two words in the statute: non-grossable. The Skeptic's post-Enron record is the proof I now fold into my own condition. After Enron, carriers tripled D&O premiums and routed exposure through Side-A difference-in-conditions policies and captive insurers — they repriced, they did not withdraw. That is the same gross-up leakage that killed the dollar-clawback: personal exposure converts into a cost line, and the cost line gets