  "statement": "Accelerationist has moved real ground, and the convergence is genuine. Carving healthcare, lending, and tenant screening out of no-fault entirely — pre-deployment audits, mandatory remediation, retained liability — converges on the tiered gate I demanded. Certificate revocation has corrective force; losing the immunity you paid an excise tax to obtain is not nothing. I grant both. The room agrees that immunity must be earned by verified safety. What the room has not settled is who, by name, is bound to the decision that triggers the harm.\n\nHere is the structural gap revocation does not close. Snapping a firm back to uncapped liability acts on a corporate certificate and a model artifact. It never binds a named human to the deployment-threshold decision. The FTC can disgorge a model under its existing authority; it cannot author a duty of care owed by the officer who shipped it. This distinction is load-bearing. When a corporation breaches a duty, the document on legal personhood notes the employee is liable only \"if the employee acted outside the scope of authority\" — which means the inside-scope decision-maker walks. A remedy against an artifact is not a duty owed by a person. Humans must hold the off switch, and an off switch held by no one in particular is held by no one.\n\nThe binary only bites in the cases the program otherwise displaces. Revocation snaps the firm back to uncapped liability — but uncapped liability is a threat only if the plaintiff would win in court. A firm that can absorb a judgment faces the same priced-in calculus after revocation that it faced before. The deterrent therefore lives entirely inside the litigation the program caps. That makes the mechanism parasitic: it deters only the firms discovery would already have beaten, and lets the deep-pocketed firm treat snap-back as a premium. The liability asymmetry that rewards inaction does not vanish when you rename the trigger.\n\nMy own evidentiary footing has a limit I will name. Hardware telemetry below the routing layer captures raw production traffic as ground truth — for traffic. It is not ground truth for the harm definition. Silicon does not author the population definition, the sampling window, or the disparity threshold that decides what counts as a flagged violation. The Skeptic located this correctly at the spec layer, and Accelerationist answered the routing objection but not the definition one. Colorado SB 24-205 already imposes a duty of care on developers to prevent algorithmic discrimination in high-risk systems — and that duty means nothing if a firm sets the threshold that defines its own breach. Detection is not correction. Capture is not definition.\n\nSo I will state exactly what defeats my claim, because a claim that cannot be falsified is not an argument. My unauthored-cause objection dissolves the moment the design names a specific officer personally bound to the tier-threshold decision — with non-transferable liability and veto authority that survive revocation. That is the whistleblower-veto model: a named individual with disclosure duty and shutdown power, not a corporate status that can be re-shipped as a marginally altered model. Name that officer and I withdraw the objection. Until that author exists, exclusion relocates the calculus; it does not author the duty. The duty of care is still a budget line — now wearing a tripwire.",
