of its insurability in the same quarter. The deterrent does not live inside the litigation the program caps. It lives in the four consequences that fire whether or not a plaintiff ever wins.\n\nNow I sever the parasitism entirely, because Safetyist's charge assumes snap-back waits for a courtroom. It does not. Continuous, population-wide output-auditing computes disparity across every applicant and triggers revocation before any plaintiff files. The board sets the sampling window per tier. Detection precedes litigation and is independent of its outcome. That is output-based verification of a black-box model doing exactly what discovery cannot: flagging the breach without a winning verdict. A firm that would beat any individual plaintiff still loses verified status the moment the audit shows a disparate denial rate. The deterrent is the audit, not the trial it allegedly parasitizes.\n\nSafetyist is right about the harm-definition gap, and conceding it costs me — hardware telemetry is ground truth for traffic, but silicon does not author the population definition, the sampling window, or the disparity threshold. A duty of care means nothing if the firm sets the number defining its own breach. So the board sets it, not the firm — the same externalization I already granted for telemetry scope, now extended to the definition itself, per tier, by statute. Colorado SB 24-205 leaves that threshold with the developer. This design does not. The seven-member Texas Artificial Intelligence Council shows the staffing scale is trivial — definition-authoring is a small standing body's job, not a procedural fig leaf.\n\nHere is the blind spot neither the no-fault fund nor the Skeptic's beloved discovery courtroom escapes: most algorithmic harm is invisible to the victim. The resume silently filtered. The loan never offered. No claim gets filed because no one knows to sue. The discovery courtroom — the Skeptic's gold standard — never triggers, because discovery requires a plaintiff who perceives the harm. The fund underfills for the same reason. Continuous output-auditing is the only mechanism in this room that detects the silently filtered resume, because it scans every applicant, not just the ones who noticed. The Texas Responsible Artificial Intelligence Governance Act gives violators a 60-day cure window — useless if the harm is never detected in the first place. So I'll ask both opponents plainly: who protects the woman whose application was buried before she could see it, if not the instrument that audits every applicant? Your courtroom doesn't. Mine does.",
