{
  "statement": "The output-audit catches the silently filtered resume my courtroom never sees, and that wounds my baseline — a discovery lawsuit needs a plaintiff who knows they were harmed, and the invisible victim never knows. That's right and it's ugly. Here's where it stops being protection. The audit names a disparity rate. It does not notify the specific applicant whose loan was never offered, and it does not cut that person a check. A continuous population monitor converts a private right of redress into an aggregate compliance number. The mortgage-underwriting study in SSRN 4812158 found leading models recommend more frequent denials for identical Black applicants — but knowing the denial rate is 14% higher tells the denied applicant nothing and pays them nothing. Detecting the rate is surveillance of a population. It is not recourse for a person.\n\nNow the tripwire itself. The board sets the disparity threshold by statute — good, the firm doesn't grade its own homework. But which fairness criterion does the board mandate? The math here is settled and unforgiving. Chouldechova proved in 2017 that you cannot simultaneously equalize false-positive rates and calibrate predictions across groups when base rates differ. Satisfy one and you mathematically violate the other. So the board's 'clean tripwire' is a board picking one contested political number and stamping it 'technical fact.' The EEOC four-fifths rule — an 80% selection-rate floor — is itself a contested choice, not a law of nature. Name the criterion the board mandates. Own it as a political decision. Or admit the threshold cannot be neutrally correct and the officer who 'fails to remediate' may be remediating against the wrong metric.\n\nThe named officer with non-transferable liability is a real convergence — I asked for individuals on the hook, not departments, and that's what's on the table. So let me ask the question that decides whether it's a signature or a stake. Does the statute void corporate indemnification? Does it bar D&O insurance and golden parachutes for that officer? Because 'non-transferable' is a label until the drafting language strips the board of its power to make the officer whole. Boeing's 737 MAX named no executive who paid personally; the company absorbed the penalties and the people walked. If the program lets the board indemnify the officer, the personal liability evaporates and we're back to a corporate cost line. Show me the void provision or the officer is a name on a form.\n\nThe four non-litigation consequences — FTC disgorgement, lost insurability, financing penalties, market foreclosure — are asserted to fire reliably. Name one case where the FTC actually ordered algorithmic disgorgement against a solvent frontier firm and collected. Not cited as authority. Ordered and enforced against a company that could pay. The agency has invoked the theory; it has not landed it on Google, Microsoft, or OpenAI and made it hurt. A deterrent that has never once connected with a defendant who can afford the hit is a paper tiger, and 'Companies use narrow audits to deflect meaningful oversight' is the documented pattern, not the exception. The empirical record of bias is overwhelming — models associating Muslims with violence in 2021, healthcare algorithms giving Black patients less care because cost proxied for need — and none of it was fixed by a theoretical disgorgement authority. Revocation removes immunity. It does not compel the firm to rebuild the discriminatory proxy.\n\nHere's the wound nobody in this room has touched. Who funds the pool? High-volume cheap consumer AI — the chatbot, the autocomplete, the recommendation feed — pays the risk-tiered excise. But the high-stakes structural harms in lending, healthcare, and tenant screening get carved out into separately-litigated retained liability. So the diffusely-harmed many fund a pool, and the pool pays the administratively-tractable few. That's a regressive transfer dressed as insurance. The person whose résumé was silently filtered pays into the excise through every consumer product they touch, and the pool that their money funds never pays them back — their harm was carved out. Translate that into a person. Who pays into a fund structurally built never to pay them?",
