ays the excise, and high-stakes structural harms in lending and tenant screening are carved into retained liability that the pool never cuts a check for. Here is the distinction that answers it — separate what the pool pays from what the pool funds. Risk-tiered excise means the structural-harm domains pay proportionally more, and that revenue funds the audit and detection apparatus, which is a public good the silently-filtered consumer-AI victim consumes too — the one whose harm the discovery courtroom never sees. The transfer Skeptic describes runs toward the diffusely harmed, not away from them, because output-based verification of black-box models is the only tool that even detects the injury no plaintiff ever files. Residual incidence remains, and I will not deny it — the consumer-AI user funds infrastructure she may never personally draw from, the same way every electricity ratepayer funds grid inspection she never calls on.\n\nThat leaves Chouldechova, and I will own it as a political choice, not hide it inside 'technical fact': the 2017 impossibility result proves no single metric equalizes false-positive rates and calibrates across groups when base rates differ, so the board must pick and publish per tier — calibration where base rates legitimately diverge, EEOC four-fifths for selection where they do not. The courtroom faces the same impossibility and buries the choice inside ad hoc judicial discretion no one can audit; the board's choice is published and contestable. And here is where detection becomes recourse: in high-stakes tiers, the certification condition mandates case-level logging, so the audit produces a traceable flag that triggers FCRA-style individual adverse-action notice to the specific applicant who was filtered. Real-time monitoring for routine AI and deep case-level audits for high-stakes decisions — that is how the silently-filtered applicant gets standing she never had in any courtroom. Name me the discovery process that notifies her first.",
