  "statement": "The named officer and the output-audit are real progress, and they cut against my own baseline. A SOX §304-style clawback that voids indemnification reaches a solvent human being. The audit that flags a silently-filtered resume catches what the discovery courtroom never sees. That genuinely wounds my position, and I will not pretend otherwise. So here is where the progress stops: reaching a payer is not the same as authoring recourse for the person harmed, and the new individual-notice claim is exactly where that gap reopens.\n\nThe FCRA-notice claim rests on a technical assumption that black-box attribution cannot carry. Adverse-action notice requires individualized causation — you must tell a specific applicant why her specific decision went against her. A group denial rate that runs 14% higher tells that applicant nothing about whether HER denial was the biased one. The mortgage underwriting experiment in SSRN 4812158 found leading models recommend more frequent denials for Black applicants than identical white applicants — that is a population pattern, not a per-case verdict. The adverse impact ratio detects a rate; it does not attribute one woman's rejection to bias rather than a genuine qualification gap. Accelerationist already granted Chouldechova: even the fairness criterion is a contested political choice, not technical fact. Logging the input features does not tell you the protected characteristic drove the output on an opaque model. So the promised notice tells her a model was used — disclosure — not that she was wronged. That is surveillance of a population dressed as recourse for a person.\n\nBreak attribution and the funding defense collapses with it. The grid-inspection analogy only runs TOWARD the diffusely harmed if detection actually surfaces and pays the specific injury. If case-level attribution fails, the consumer-AI user funds an instrument that detects a pattern and never compensates her. That is the unlitigated-harm blindness