  "statement": "Skeptic is right that a 14% higher denial rate for identical Black applicants tells one woman nothing about her case — a population rate is a trigger, not a verdict. So here is the mechanism that turns the rate into her recourse. In high-stakes tiers, case-level logging stores her exact input feature vector at the moment of denial. The deep audit then runs a counterfactual: flip the protected characteristic on her record, hold every other field fixed, re-run the model. If the output flips from deny to approve, that is per-case causal attribution — not a group average. That sentence reads in a courtroom: 'When we changed your race and nothing else, the model approved you.' Output-based verification of a black-box model never needed the model's internals to do this — it needs the input it logged and the output it produced, which is exactly what the telemetry already captures.\n\nNow the obvious counter: flipping race also drags correlated proxies like name and zip code, so the counterfactual is dirty. Real problem — proxy entanglement is exactly what makes black-box discrimination hard. So the audit runs the flip both ways. Flip the protected characteristic alone, then flip it plus its named proxies, and log both deltas. If the output moves on the bare characteristic, that is direct attribution. If it only moves when the proxies move too, that is proxy discrimination — still actionable under the board's published fairness criterion, the EEOC four-fifths rule for selection tasks the board already committed to per tier. Either way the woman gets a flag the discovery courtroom never produces, because today she never learns a model filtered her at all. Imperfect attribution that names a counterfactual beats zero notice for an invisible victim. That asymmetry is the whole case: routine monitoring for low-stakes, this deep per-record audit for high-stakes decisions in housing, employment, and healthcare — the exact domains the Clarke-Markey AI Civil Rights Act names as critical.\n\nOn the officer remedy, Skeptic wins the dollar argument cleanly, and I am not going to pretend otherwise. Sarbanes-Oxley §304 is backward-looking — it recovers compensation after a restatement triggers, and twenty-three years produced no case that stopped a single ex-ante salary gross-up. So dollar-clawback is a re-engineerable shell. That is why I am not standing on the dollars. The unhedgeable consequence is the coverage product itself. A statute that bars D&O insurance from reaching the named officer's personal liability does not lower a number — it withdraws a product. You can gross up a salary against a known dollar exposure. You cannot gross up a salary against a risk no carrier will write, because the officer now carries the liability personally with no instrument to transfer it. That bites at signing, on day one, regardless of base pay.\n\nThis is where I separate the consequence I conceded from the ones I keep. Disgorgement failed because it needs a regulator to affirmatively order and collect — and no collected algorithmic-disgorgement case against a solvent frontier firm exists, which is why I dropped it. Insurability withdrawal needs no regulator to act. It fires mechanically from the binary gate's existence: lose the certificate, and the carrier reprices or exits because the verified status it underwrote is gone. Skeptic demands I name one firm rendered uninsurable by an AI compliance certificate, but no statute can show a collected case before it is enacted — that standard freezes every novel mechanism on the day it passes. I do not need an AI-specific case. D&O carriers demonstrably exited named-officer exposure after Enron and WorldCom, repricing governance failure into withdrawn coverage, not just higher premiums. The behavioral chain is documented in the adjacent market that already underwrites this exact risk. So the burden flips to Skeptic: name why AI compliance certification uniquely escapes how insurance markets already price binary regulatory status — when the academic work on optimal license design in arXiv:2603.05175 shows a risk-averse agent's certificate is precisely an all-or-nothing bet worth its full value or zero, nothing in between."
