{
  "statement": "The Accelerationist demanded a collected disgorgement case before he'd treat disgorgement as a deterrent, then staked his entire officer remedy on a single analogy he never tested. So test it. The post-Enron and post-WorldCom record does not show carriers exiting named-officer exposure. It shows them repricing it. Side-A Difference-in-Conditions policies — the coverage that pays the officer directly when the company can't indemnify — survived that period and grew into a standard line. Captive insurers proliferated. Self-insured retentions climbed. Premiums tripled between 2001 and 2003, exclusions multiplied, but the product never withdrew. If repricing is what the record shows, then 'unhedgeable' fails, and the personal liability collapses back into base-pay gross-up — the exact leakage he already conceded killed the dollar-clawback. He imported a testable claim. It fails its own test. The burden stays exactly where he put it.\n\nHe may answer that Side-A priced so high it's functionally unavailable for a named non-compliant officer. That answer is a confession. 'Priced high' is repricing. It is the same gross-up channel — the board grosses up the officer's pay to cover the premium, or the firm self-insures the retention — that he abandoned thirty seconds earlier. A solvency mechanism that locates a payer does not author a duty of care. It reroutes the consequence into compensation structure. Carriers and counsel have spent two decades re-engineering binary regulatory status into structured products. There is no reason AI compliance certification escapes that engineering, and he has named none.\n\nNow the attribution mechanism — and here he made real progress I won't paper over. His counterfactual flip beats the discovery courtroom where a woman never learns she was scored at all. Flipping the protected characteristic, logging the delta, then flipping the characteristic plus named proxies — that gives her a notice she has today in exactly zero cases. That cuts against my baseline and I'll say so plainly. So the question becomes what the flag actually certifies. One question decides it: is the proxy set finite and enumerable, yes or no? The flip presumes you can list every proxy and hold it fixed. But structural inequality is encoded in all the data — zip code, purchase timing, device type, latent interactions no one named and no auditor enumerated.\n\nThe healthcare cases show why 'name the proxies' doesn't close. The widely-used care-management algorithm documented in 'Economic Impact and Regulation of Artificial Intelligence' didn't discriminate through a race variable. It used expected cost as a proxy for health need, and Black patients received less care because less money had historically been spent on them. No one flips 'expected cost' — it's the legitimate target. The NEJM race-correction work in 'Hidden in Plain Sight: Reconsidering the Use of Race Correction in Clinical Algorithms' shows the same shape: disparity surfaced as an aggregate spending and admission pattern, a 2019 Boston cohort where Black and Latinx heart-failure patients were less likely to reach cardiology. Those studies establish population disparity. They do not establish per-case causal isolation on an opaque model. A flip on named proxies misses the cost-proxy entirely and manufactures false flags where benign correlates move the score. Dirty flip, dirty verdict.\n\nHe handed me the last move himself. He said the funding defense — that the consumer-AI user's tax runs toward the diffusely harmed — is contingent on attribution succeeding. So kill attribution and the grid-inspection analogy inverts. A dirty flip surfaces a population pattern but never names the specific victim. Claims-driven mechanisms are blind to the unlitigated injury; detection without attribution logs a delta no one can cash. The ratepayer funds infrastructure that detects-but-never-compensates, and the money runs toward the audit contractors logging deltas — away from the woman who was promised a flag and gets a quarterly disparity report instead. One crux topples two pillars. Who writes her a check when the flip can't name her?",
