  "statement": "I concede the counterfactual flip gives the high-stakes-tier applicant a notice she gets in exactly zero cases today, and against my own baseline that is real progress—but only when the harm rides a named characteristic. I still hold that reaching a solvent payer is not authoring a duty of care: the D&O exposure gets repriced, not withdrawn, and a flip blind to latent proxies never names the woman it disparaged. I would change on insurability if a non-grossable conduct exclusion falls personally on the named officer in the misconduct case, and I would change on attribution if someone builds a method that isolates per-case causation across the full feature space, not just the proxies a developer thought to enumerate.\n\nThe sharpest disagreement with the Accelerationist is one empirical fact: after Enron and WorldCom, carriers repriced named-officer exposure—Side-A DIC coverage, captive insurers, climbing retentions, premiums that tripled—they did not exit the market. Priced high is repriced, not withdrawn. That repricing routes through the exact gross-up channel the Accelerationist already conceded turns the dollar-clawback into a re-engineerable shell. The binary gate's existence does not make withdrawal automatic; it makes withdrawal a pricing decision, and pricing decisions get passed downstream until the consequence is paid by everyone except the person who authored the cause.\n\nThe attribution gap is the bounding case, and healthcare is where it bites hardest. The widely-used care-management algorithm in 'Economic Impact and Regulation of Artificial Intelligence' used expected cost as a proxy for health need, and that single choice sent Black patients less care than white patients—nobody named race anywhere in the model. A flip that toggles enumerated characteristics catches none of that, because the discrimination rode a latent proxy the auditor never listed. The NEJM record on race-correction shows the same pattern: researchers decided race adjustment was essential without understanding the causal mechanism. A 14% group disparity certifies a rate, not her verdict—the flag tells the woman a population pattern exists and tells her nothing about whether the machine wronged her.\n\nWho writes her a check when the flip can't name her? Nobody does. A claims-driven, excise-funded pool detects the population delta, logs it, and pays the audit contractors who logged it—and the diffusely-harmed woman never files, because the program that was supposed to find her never named her. A fund that detects-but-never-compensates is a regressive transfer: it taxes to surface a disparity and routes the money to the people measuring it, while entrenching the exact injustice it was built to correct. So the question I'll leave open: if the program's own design pays the auditors before it pays the victim, what is the excise tax actually insuring?",
